# Section 2.2: The Main Method

 
 🚀 Entry Point 
 ⏱️ Estimated Time: 20 minutes 
 

---

## 🎯 The Master Switch

 
 
 
 ✦ Ignition: The static void Main Entry Point 
 Every C# program is like a dormant machine waiting for a spark. While your project might contain thousands of files and millions of lines of code, the computer doesn't just read them all at once. It looks for one specific ignition switch : the static void Main method. Without this 'Master Switch,' the engine never starts. 
 
 
 
 
 
 

Every C# program has exactly **one** starting point—the Main method. When you run your program, the .NET runtime doesn't randomly pick a line to execute. It searches for a very specific method signature and begins there.

 
 ⚡ 
 
 The Entry Point Rule 
 The runtime looks for a method named exactly Main (capital M). If it doesn't find it, your program won't run—no matter how much other code you've written. 
 
 

---

## The Anatomy of the Switch

 
 ‹ 
 
 
 static 
 The 'Master' modifier. It means the switch exists on the machine itself (the class), not on a specific part (an object). The runtime can flip this switch without needing to 'build' anything else first. 
 
 
 void 
 The return type. It tells the machine: 'Once you've run the sequence, you don't need to hand anything back to me.' You can also use int if the machine needs to report a success or failure code. 
 
 
 Main 
 The specific name. This isn't just a label; it's a hard-coded requirement. The .NET Runtime (the operator) is literally programmed to look for this exact word to start the process. 
 
 
 string[] args 
 The 'Input Dials.' These allow you to pass external instructions into the machine at the moment of ignition (e.g., file paths or configuration settings). 
 
 
